# The EM algorithm {#sec-em}

Recall the consistency argument of @sec-consistency in @sec-mle: maximum likelihood is
worth chasing. But when each observation hides a latent label, the likelihood is a sum
inside a logarithm and has no closed-form maximizer. Expectation maximization sidesteps
this by optimizing a lower bound instead, one whose maximizer we can write down.

## The evidence lower bound {#sec-elbo}

For any distribution $q$ over the latent variables,

$$ \log p(x) \;\ge\; \mathbb{E}_{q}\!\left[\log \frac{p(x, z)}{q(z)}\right] \;=:\; \mathcal{L}(q, \theta) $$ {#eq-elbo}

The gap is exactly $\mathrm{KL}\!\left(q \,\|\, p(z \mid x)\right)$, so the bound is tight
when $q$ is the posterior.

::: {.callout-note collapse="true" title="Why the gap is a divergence"}
Start from $\log p(x) = \mathbb{E}_q[\log p(x)]$ and substitute
$p(x) = p(x, z) / p(z \mid x)$. Adding and subtracting $\log q(z)$ inside the expectation
splits the logarithm into $\mathcal{L}(q, \theta)$ plus
$\mathrm{KL}(q \,\|\, p(z \mid x))$, and the divergence is non-negative by Gibbs'
inequality.
:::

The E-step sets $q$ to the current posterior over the latents; every responsibility is a
posterior mean in the sense of @eq-expectation. The M-step then maximizes the bound in
closed form. Here is that update:

```python
def m_step(x, resp):
    w = resp.sum(axis=0)
    mu = (resp.T @ x) / w[:, None]
    return mu, w / w.sum()
```

Line by line: summing the responsibilities down the columns gives each component's
**effective count**, the soft number of points it currently owns. Each component **mean**
is then re-estimated as a responsibility-weighted average of the data, so points a
component is confident about pull its mean hardest. The new **mixing weights** are just
the normalized counts, and the updated means are returned for the next E-step.

A quick numerical sanity check that the counts sum to the number of points:

```{python}
#| echo: true
import numpy as np
resp = np.array([[0.9, 0.1], [0.2, 0.8], [0.6, 0.4]])
print("effective counts:", resp.sum(axis=0), "-> total", resp.sum())
```

By @eq-elbo each round can only raise the bound, so EM climbs toward a local optimum of
the very likelihood @sec-consistency told us to trust.
